WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.505
The first published NIST PQC standards are not the end of the migration story.

00:00:04.505 --> 00:00:08.660
Additional algorithms remain important because cryptographic migration is a long program,

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:09.980
not a single product upgrade.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.480
Falcon was selected as an additional digital signature algorithm path,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.460
and NIST has continued work toward a separate standard for it.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.010
The standardized naming and final implementation expectations should be tracked as

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.460
guidance matures.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.615
HQC was selected as a backup key-establishment algorithm.

00:00:24.615 --> 00:00:28.490
Backup choices matter because organizations should not rely on a single design family

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.580
forever when the ecosystem is still maturing.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:35.260
A backup KEM can provide another option if future analysis, implementation experience,

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.860
or operational requirements create a need for diversity beyond the primary

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.900
key-establishment standard.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.820
Primary choices, backup choices, and future standards play different roles.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.305
A primary standard is the main reference point for near-term implementation and vendor

00:00:48.305 --> 00:00:48.460
support.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.419
A backup choice helps manage longer-term risk and diversity.

00:00:51.419 --> 00:00:54.900
A future standard may become relevant as products, validation programs,

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.580
and protocol support develop.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.785
Treating every status as identical creates confusion.

00:00:59.785 --> 00:01:03.100
Migration programs should track these algorithms without chasing every draft as if it were

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.420
already production-ready.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.655
The right posture is informed readiness: know what is coming, ask vendors about roadmaps,

00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:10.880
design systems that can change algorithms,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.415
and avoid hard-coding assumptions that will make the next transition expensive.

00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:18.830
PQC readiness is partly about adopting standards and partly about building the ability to

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:19.300
adapt.
